# Human factors and aeronautical decision-making: PHAK (FAA-H-8083-25C) chapters 2 and 17, AIM chapter 8, Risk Management Handbook (FAA-H-8083-2A). ACS area I.H and the risk management elements of every task.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Hypoxia", "Which type of hypoxia results from carbon monoxide poisoning?",
      ["Hypemic hypoxia", "Hypoxic hypoxia", "Histotoxic hypoxia"],
      "Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin so the blood cannot carry enough oxygen, which is hypemic hypoxia. Hypoxic hypoxia comes from low oxygen pressure at altitude, and histotoxic from alcohol or drugs that stop cells using oxygen."),
    S(1, 2, "Hazardous attitudes", "A pilot thinks, \"Rules are for other people; I know this airplane better than the POH.\" Which hazardous attitude is this, and what is its antidote?",
      ["Anti-authority; follow the rules, they are usually right",
       "Macho; taking chances is foolish",
       "Invulnerability; it could happen to me too"],
      "Resenting rules and procedures is the anti-authority attitude; its antidote is \"Follow the rules. They are usually right.\""),
    S(1, 2, "Spatial disorientation", "In instrument conditions a pilot feels a strong sense of turning and climbing. What should the pilot do?",
      ["Trust the flight instruments and fly by them",
       "Trust the inner ear sensations and correct for them",
       "Close one eye and look for a horizon outside"],
      "Without outside references the vestibular system gives false signals. The cure for spatial disorientation is to rely on, and fly by, the flight instruments."),
    S(1, 2, "Night vision", "What is the best way to see objects at night?",
      ["Scan slowly and look slightly to one side of the object",
       "Look directly at the object and keep the gaze fixed on it",
       "Turn the cockpit lights up to full brightness to help scanning"],
      "Rods, which work best in dim light, are around the edge of the retina. Off-center viewing and a slow scan with short pauses let the rods pick up objects. Bright cockpit lights spoil dark adaptation."),
    S(1, 3, "Landing illusions", "On approach to a runway narrower than you are used to, what illusion may occur?",
      ["You feel higher than you are, and may fly a lower approach",
       "You feel lower than you are, and may fly a higher approach",
       "You feel slower than you are, and may land too fast"],
      "A narrow runway looks like a higher approach, so the pilot tends to descend too low. A wide runway gives the opposite illusion of being too low."),
    S(1, 1, "Collision avoidance", "Another aircraft stays in the same spot on your windscreen and seems to grow larger. What does this mean?",
      ["It is probably on a collision course with you",
       "It is moving away from you on a parallel path",
       "It is overtaking you on a converging path that will clear"],
      "An aircraft with no apparent relative motion that slowly grows is on a collision course and can stay hidden until very close. Take evasive action at once."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 2, "Hyperventilation", "A passenger becomes anxious, breathes rapidly and feels lightheaded with tingling fingers. What should you do?",
      ["Have them slow their breathing rate, or talk aloud, to restore carbon dioxide",
       "Have them breathe faster and more deeply to take in more oxygen",
       "Climb higher so that the lower air pressure slows and calms their breathing"],
      "These are signs of hyperventilation, too much carbon dioxide breathed off. Slowing the breathing, breathing into a bag or talking aloud restores the balance."),
    S(2, 2, "Hazardous attitudes", "A pilot who feels that nothing they do will change the outcome of a deteriorating situation shows which hazardous attitude?",
      ["Resignation", "Impulsivity", "Invulnerability"],
      "Resignation is the feeling of \"What's the use?\" Its antidote is \"I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.\""),
    S(2, 2, "Scuba diving", "According to the AIM, how long should a pilot wait to fly to a cabin altitude of 8,000 feet or less after a dive that did not require a controlled ascent?",
      ["At least 12 hours", "At least 4 hours", "At least 48 hours"],
      "The AIM recommends at least 12 hours after a non-decompression dive for flights up to 8,000 feet cabin altitude, and 24 hours after a decompression dive or for flights above 8,000 feet."),
    S(2, 3, "Somatogravic illusion", "During a rapid acceleration on takeoff into a dark night, a pilot may feel",
      ["the nose is pitching up, and push the nose down too far",
       "the nose is pitching down, and pull the nose up too far",
       "a turn to the left, and roll the wings to the right"],
      "A sudden forward acceleration stimulates the inner ear like a pitch-up. Without a visual horizon the pilot may push forward into a dangerous nose-low attitude."),
    S(2, 1, "Personal checklist", "What is the IMSAFE checklist used for?",
      ["Checking the pilot's own fitness before flight",
       "Checking the airplane's required documents",
       "Checking the weather against personal minimums"],
      "IMSAFE stands for Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue and Emotion (or Eating). It is a personal fitness check before every flight."),
    S(2, 3, "Carbon monoxide", "On a winter flight with the cabin heat on, you get a headache and feel drowsy. What should you do first?",
      ["Turn off the heater, open the fresh air vents and use oxygen if available",
       "Turn up the heater to stay warm, then descend to a lower altitude",
       "Close all the cabin vents to keep exhaust fumes from the outside air out of the cabin"],
      "Headache and drowsiness with the heater on suggest carbon monoxide leaking from the exhaust through the heat muff. Shut off the heat, ventilate, use oxygen if you have it and land as soon as practical."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Hypoxia", "Which statement about hypoxia is true?",
      ["Its symptoms vary from person to person and can begin without warning",
       "It affects only pilots who fly above 18,000 feet MSL without supplemental oxygen",
       "Its first symptom is always a strong feeling of breathlessness"],
      "Hypoxia's onset is insidious: symptoms differ between people and often start with euphoria or impaired judgment rather than discomfort. It can affect pilots at much lower altitudes, especially at night."),
    S(0, 2, "Hazardous attitudes", "Which antidote matches the impulsivity hazardous attitude?",
      ["Not so fast. Think first.", "Taking chances is foolish.", "It could happen to me."],
      "Impulsivity is the need to do something, anything, immediately. Its antidote is \"Not so fast. Think first.\""),
    S(0, 2, "Landing illusions", "Rain on the windscreen during an approach can make a pilot think the airplane is",
      ["higher than it is, leading to a low approach",
       "lower than it is, leading to a high approach",
       "slower than it is, leading to a fast landing"],
      "Water on the windscreen bends light and can create the illusion of being too high, so the pilot may fly a lower approach. Haze can have a similar effect."),
    S(0, 2, "Graveyard spiral", "After a prolonged, coordinated descending turn in clouds, a pilot levels the wings and feels a turn in the opposite direction. If the pilot rolls back into the original turn, what can result?",
      ["A graveyard spiral", "A Coriolis illusion", "An inversion illusion"],
      "The inner ear stops sensing a long turn. On leveling, it signals a turn the other way, and banking back into the old turn with back pressure tightens a descending spiral."),
    S(0, 2, "Night vision", "How long does it take the eyes to become fully adapted to darkness?",
      ["About 30 minutes", "About 5 minutes", "About 2 to 3 hours"],
      "Rods take about 30 minutes to adapt fully to darkness, and a single bright light can undo it. Use dim red or white cockpit lighting."),
    S(0, 2, "Decision-making", "In the 3P model of risk management, what are the three steps?",
      ["Perceive, process and perform", "Plan, practice and perform", "Prepare, predict and proceed"],
      "The pilot perceives hazards, processes them to judge the risk, and performs by acting to reduce the risk, then repeats the cycle throughout the flight."),
    S(0, 2, "Risk assessment", "In the PAVE checklist, what do the letters stand for?",
      ["Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment and External pressures",
       "Preflight, Altitude, Visibility and Equipment on board",
       "Planning, Airspace, Vectors and Emergencies"],
      "PAVE groups the risk factors of a flight: the pilot, the aircraft, the environment (weather, terrain, airports) and external pressures such as schedules and passengers."),
    S(0, 3, "Coriolis illusion", "Which action is most likely to cause the overwhelming tumbling sensation of the Coriolis illusion?",
      ["Moving the head abruptly during a prolonged constant-rate turn",
       "Climbing steadily at a constant rate while flying a straight course",
       "Accelerating quickly in level flight during a takeoff at night"],
      "After a sustained turn the inner ear fluid stops moving relative to the canals. An abrupt head movement then sets fluid moving in another canal, giving a strong sense of rotation on an axis that does not exist."),
    S(0, 1, "Fatigue and stress", "Which is a sign that fatigue is affecting a pilot's performance?",
      ["Missing radio calls and making small, careless errors",
       "Feeling more alert and confident than at the start of the day",
       "Scanning the instruments faster than normal and more widely"],
      "Fatigue shows up as slower reactions, narrowed attention, missed calls and careless mistakes. The only cure is rest."),
]
